1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:19,000
■

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
字幕来源：CMCT

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
后期：筆孞·Lee    Sung1219

4
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:27,310
Excuse me.

5
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:35,900
I beg your pardon. Aren't you Guy Haines?

6
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:37,690
Oh, sure.

7
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:41,110
I saw you blast Faraday right off the court in South Orange last season.

8
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:44,120
- Made the semifinals, didn't you? - Yeah.

9
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:47,750
Oh, I certainly admire people who do things.

10
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:53,500
By the way, my name is Bruno. Bruno Anthony. See?

11
00:02:55,130 --> 00:02:57,380
It's corny, but my mother gave it to me...

12
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:00,010
- so I wear it to please her. - How do you do?

13
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:02,430
I don't talk much. You go ahead and read.

14
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,010
Thanks.

15
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:10,100
You know, it must be pretty exciting to be so important.

16
00:03:10,310 --> 00:03:14,020
- Tennis player isn't so important. - But people who do things are.

17
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:16,730
Me, I never do anything.

18
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:19,650
I suppose you're going to Southampton for the doubles.

19
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:21,320
You are a tennis fan.

20
00:03:21,610 --> 00:03:25,530
Gee, I wish I could go watch you, but I've gotta get back to Washington.

21
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:27,410
I'm from Arlington, you know.

22
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,920
- Cigarette? - No, thanks. I don't smoke much.

23
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:36,540
Me, I smoke too much.

24
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,050
Oh, here.

25
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:40,670
Oh, thanks.

26
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:43,180
Elegant.

27
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:49,640
"From A to G." I'll bet I can guess who "A" is.

28
00:03:49,850 --> 00:03:51,480
- Yeah? - Anne Morton.

29
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:56,560
You see, sometimes I turn the sports page, and I see the society section.

30
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:59,480
And the pictures. She's very beautiful.

31
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:01,780
Senator Morton's daughter, huh?

32
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:04,320
You're quite a reader, Mr. Anthony.

33
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:06,500
Yes, I am. Ask me anything.

34
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:07,830
I got the answer.

35
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:09,700
Even about people I don't know.

36
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:13,660
Like who would like to marry whom...

37
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:17,550
when his wife gets her divorce.

38
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:19,550
Perhaps you read too much.

39
00:04:19,710 --> 00:04:24,130
Oh, there I go again. Too friendly. It always happens.

40
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:28,390
I meet somebody who I like and admire, and I open my mouth too much.

41
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,890
That's all right, forget it. I guess I'm a little jittery.

42
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:34,190
Oh, there's a new cure for that.

43
00:04:34,390 --> 00:04:36,310
- Waiter. - Yes, sir.

44
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:40,730
Scotch and plain water, please. A pair. Doubles.

45
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:42,900
The only kind of doubles I play.

46
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:44,490
You'll have to drink both.

47
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:46,200
And I can do it.

48
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:49,490
When's the wedding?

49
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:52,330
- What? - The wedding. You and Anne Morton.

50
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,750
It was in the papers.

51
00:04:53,910 --> 00:04:57,330
It shouldn't have been. Unless they legalized bigamy overnight.

52
00:04:58,170 --> 00:05:01,250
Oh, I've got a wonderful theory about that.

53
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:03,550
Someday I'll tell you about it.

54
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:08,220
But right now, I suppose divorce is, well, the simplest operation.

55
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,560
It's wonderful having you as company all the way to New York.

56
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:17,270
I'm not going direct. I'm stopping off at Metcalf.

57
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:20,650
Metcalf? Who would want to stop off at Metcalf?

58
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:22,440
Well, it's my hometown.

59
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:27,280
Oh, I get it. A little chat with your wife about the divorce.

60
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:29,160
Close enough.

61
00:05:29,410 --> 00:05:30,870
Thanks. I think I will.

62
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:33,540
Well, here's luck.

63
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:37,460
Drink up, and we'll have lunch sent to my compartment.

64
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:42,460
Thanks, but I'll go to the dining car. Oh, waiter.

65
00:05:43,630 --> 00:05:44,910
Are there any seats in the diner?

66
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:47,630
Not for about 20 minutes, sir.

67
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:49,640
You'll have to lunch with me.

68
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:55,470
here's to the next Mrs. Haines.

69
00:06:01,150 --> 00:06:05,280
Sure, I went to college. Got kicked out of three of them.

70
00:06:05,490 --> 00:06:07,570
Drinking and gambling.

71
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:10,280
Not like you, huh? All right, so I'm a bum.

72
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:12,830
Who said you were?

73
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:14,620
My father.

74
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,500
He hates me.

75
00:06:17,830 --> 00:06:18,730
With all his money,

76
00:06:18,730 --> 00:06:21,580
he thinks I ought to catch the 8:05 bus

77
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:22,330
every morning...

78
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:26,340
punch a time clock and work my way up selling paint or something.

79
00:06:26,510 --> 00:06:28,630
What do you think of a man like that?

80
00:06:29,510 --> 00:06:33,600
- Well, I think possibly... - Yes, I hate him too.

81
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:38,020
I tell you, I get so sore at him sometimes...

82
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:39,850
I want to kill him.

83
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:44,690
- I don't think you know what you want. - Well, I want to do something.

84
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:46,530
Everything.

85
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:51,950
You know, I've got a theory that you should do everything before you die.

86
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:57,790
Have you ever driven a car blindfolded at 150 miles an hour?

87
00:06:58,710 --> 00:07:00,370
Not lately.

88
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:03,500
I did. I flew in a jet plane too.

89
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:08,840
Man, that's a thrill. Almost blew the sawdust out of my head.

90
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:14,550
And I'm going to make a reservation on the first rocket to the moon.

91
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:18,680
- What are you trying to prove? - Well, I'm not like you.

92
00:07:18,890 --> 00:07:20,980
You're lucky. You're smart.

93
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:23,610
Marrying the boss' daughter makes

94
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:26,570
a nice shortcut to a career, doesn't it?

95
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,570
Marrying the senator's daughter has nothing to do with it.

96
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:34,410
Can't I look beyond a tennis net without being out for something?

97
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:36,720
Take it easy. I'm your friend,

98
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:37,870
remember? I like you.

99
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:39,830
I'd do anything for you.

100
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,410
Sure, Bruno.

101
00:07:41,670 --> 00:07:44,540
We'll be pulling in soon. I've got to change trains.

102
00:07:44,790 --> 00:07:47,670
What did you say her name was? Your wife's?

103
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:49,920
- Miriam. - Miriam, that's it.

104
00:07:50,170 --> 00:07:52,220
Miriam Joyce Haines.

105
00:07:52,930 --> 00:07:54,840
I suppose she played around a lot.

106
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:56,600
Skip it, Bruno.

107
00:07:56,810 --> 00:07:59,930
It's painful for a man to discover he's been a chump.

108
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:02,600
Okay.

109
00:08:05,860 --> 00:08:09,360
Wanna hear one of my ideas for a perfect murder?

110
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:11,940
The "light socket in the bathroom"...

111
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:14,860
or the "carbon monoxide in the garage"?

112
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:17,900
Neither one. I may be old-fashioned,

113
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:19,290
but murder is against the law.

114
00:08:20,450 --> 00:08:22,070
What is a life or two, Guy?

115
00:08:22,070 --> 00:08:23,830
Some people are better off dead.

116
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:27,080
Well, like your wife and my father, for instance.

117
00:08:27,290 --> 00:08:32,090
Oh, that reminds me of a wonderful idea I had once.

118
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:36,840
I used to put myself to sleep at night figuring it out.

119
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:41,930
Now, let's say that you'd like to get rid of your wife.

120
00:08:42,850 --> 00:08:45,810
- That's a morbid thought. - No, no, no. Just suppose.

121
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:47,730
Let's say you had a good reason.

122
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:51,230
- No, let's not... - No, no. Let's say.

123
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:55,150
You'd be afraid to kill her. You know why. You'd get caught.

124
00:08:55,410 --> 00:08:56,910
And what would trip you up?

125
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:58,950
The motive.

126
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:01,830
Ah... Now, here's my idea.

127
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,660
I haven't got time to listen.

128
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:06,500
Listen. It's so simple too.

129
00:09:06,750 --> 00:09:10,000
Two fellows meet accidentally, like you and me.

130
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:13,170
No connection. Never saw each other before.

131
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:17,300
Each one has somebody that he'd like to get rid of.

132
00:09:19,180 --> 00:09:20,640
So. they swap murders.

133
00:09:20,850 --> 00:09:22,930
Swap murders?

134
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:24,850
Each does the other's murder.

135
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:26,580
Then there's nothing to connect them.

136
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:29,100
Each one has murdered a stranger.

137
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:32,520
Like you do my murder, I do yours.

138
00:09:33,190 --> 00:09:34,780
We're coming into my station.

139
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:39,450
For example, your wife, my father. Crisscross.

140
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:40,910
What?

141
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:43,370
We do talk the same language, don't we?

142
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:46,500
Sure, we talk the same language. Thanks for lunch.

143
00:09:46,710 --> 00:09:50,460
I'm glad you enjoyed it. The lamb chops were a little overdone.

144
00:09:50,630 --> 00:09:51,880
Nice meeting you.

145
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:54,640
Now, you think my theory's okay, Guy?

146
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:55,550
I mean, you like it?

147
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,970
Sure, Bruno, sure.

148
00:09:58,180 --> 00:09:59,640
They're all okay.

149
00:10:19,530 --> 00:10:21,530
Crisscross.

150
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:45,700
Hi, Bill.

151
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:47,890
Guy Haines! Good to see you, boy.

152
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:50,020
You better win at Southampton.

153
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:51,100
I got 2 bucks on you.

154
00:10:51,270 --> 00:10:54,480
- Park these in a lucky spot, will you? - Sure thing.

155
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:20,300
One-fifty, 2, 3, 4, 5. There you are.

156
00:11:20,510 --> 00:11:22,630
- Thank you very much. - You're welcome.

157
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:27,640
- Hello, Guy. - You're looking well, Miriam.

158
00:11:27,930 --> 00:11:29,640
So are you.

159
00:11:29,810 --> 00:11:33,150
Got a nice tan playing tennis with all your rich friends.

160
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:37,820
What time do we meet your lawyer?

161
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:41,240
- What's your hurry? - My hurry?

162
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:43,320
That's funny, coming from you.

163
00:11:43,530 --> 00:11:46,240
You're the one who's in a hurry, aren't you?

164
00:11:46,410 --> 00:11:48,660
When you wouldn't give me the divorce...

165
00:11:48,830 --> 00:11:50,120
I sort of hoped it was

166
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:51,660
because you were jealous.

167
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:56,420
I got over being jealous a long time ago, Miriam.

168
00:11:59,510 --> 00:12:01,010
Let's talk in here.

169
00:12:11,640 --> 00:12:12,950
Well, this is cozier.

170
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:15,190
Sort of like old times, isn't it?

171
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:17,060
Oh, skip it, Miriam.

172
00:12:17,270 --> 00:12:20,530
It's pretty late to start flirting with a discarded husband.

173
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:23,990
Especially when you're going to have another man's baby.

174
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,870
You know, I think you're handsomer than ever.

175
00:12:27,030 --> 00:12:29,370
Let's see your lawyer and get this over with.

176
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:31,790
You bring the money? Lawyers are expensive.

177
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:33,330
Yes.

178
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:40,130
Here it is.

179
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:45,220
If I'd known what all that tennis nonsense of yours would lead to...

180
00:12:45,510 --> 00:12:47,550
I wouldn't have run out on you.

181
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:49,810
What are you trying to say, Miriam?

182
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:52,890
I'm not getting a divorce.

183
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:57,100
You little double-crosser! I didn't want this divorce. You did.

184
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:59,400
You've been harping about it all year!

185
00:12:59,690 --> 00:13:02,110
It's a woman's privilege to change her mind.

186
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,030
Now I can buy me some pretty new clothes.

187
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:09,240
I wouldn't want you to be ashamed of me in Washington...

188
00:13:09,490 --> 00:13:11,910
when we go to all those swanky parties.

189
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:15,250
- What do you mean by that? - Don't look so mad, Guy.

190
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:18,380
You smile when your picture's taken for the newspaper.

191
00:13:18,590 --> 00:13:20,710
Especially with Anne Morton on your arm.

192
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:22,750
Let's not talk about Anne Morton.

193
00:13:23,050 --> 00:13:25,590
It's serious between you two, huh?

194
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:29,220
You can throw all your dreams about her into the ashcan.

195
00:13:29,430 --> 00:13:32,060
- I'm coming to Washington. - What for?

196
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:34,000
To have my baby and be with you.

197
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,140
Why me? It's not my baby.

198
00:13:36,390 --> 00:13:39,900
Yes, but people don't know that, Guy. Do they?

199
00:13:40,150 --> 00:13:44,650
Make a pretty story, the senator's daughter involved with a married man.

200
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,200
- Especially when he's to be a father. - You conniving liar!

201
00:13:48,410 --> 00:13:50,070
Keep your voice down.

202
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:54,070
What happened? He run out on you?

203
00:13:54,070 --> 00:13:56,660
No man runs out on me. Not even you.

204
00:13:56,920 --> 00:13:59,500
You've been trying to get rid of me long enough.

205
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:03,050
Now I'll go you one better. I never wanna see or hear you again.

206
00:14:03,210 --> 00:14:08,300
I could be pathetic as the deserted mother in court. Think it over.

207
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,600
- Who would believe you? - I'm warning you.

208
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:22,480
That's what should happen to people like you. I'd like to find...

209
00:14:22,690 --> 00:14:27,240
Break it up, folks. This isn't the place for a family quarrel.

210
00:14:27,490 --> 00:14:29,070
I'm sorry.

211
00:14:30,490 --> 00:14:32,160
I'm leaving.

212
00:14:32,330 --> 00:14:34,160
You heard what I said, Guy Haines.

213
00:14:34,330 --> 00:14:38,000
You can't throw me away! I'm coming to Washington to have my baby!

214
00:14:38,250 --> 00:14:40,210
- Tell that to the Senate! - Please.

215
00:14:42,090 --> 00:14:43,460
Anne?

216
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:45,960
Anne, darling, I...

217
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:49,300
Yes, I'm in Metcalf.

218
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:53,100
No, everything didn't go smoothly.

219
00:14:53,350 --> 00:14:56,270
She doesn't want the divorce. Not now.

220
00:14:58,730 --> 00:15:00,350
It's unbelievable.

221
00:15:03,150 --> 00:15:05,770
Yes, I know how you must feel.

222
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:09,820
But you sound so savage, Guy.

223
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:13,160
Sure, I sound savage. I feel savage. I'd like to break her neck.

224
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:18,700
I said, I'd like to break her foul, useless little neck.

225
00:15:24,170 --> 00:15:25,540
What's that?

226
00:15:25,710 --> 00:15:28,550
I said, I could strangle her!

227
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:33,890
I do wish you'd keep your hands quiet.

228
00:15:34,180 --> 00:15:36,260
You're so restless lately.

229
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:38,470
I like them to look just right.

230
00:15:39,390 --> 00:15:40,890
Did I file them too short?

231
00:15:41,230 --> 00:15:44,770
Oh, no, Ma. They're just fine. Thanks.

232
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:50,570
- What's the matter? - I'm all right. Don't worry about me.

233
00:15:51,030 --> 00:15:53,530
But you look so pale, dear.

234
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:55,240
Are you out of vitamins?

235
00:15:56,910 --> 00:15:58,660
I took a bottle yesterday, Ma.

236
00:15:59,410 --> 00:16:01,870
A whole fifth.

237
00:16:02,540 --> 00:16:06,290
Oh, but you have that look, dear. I always can tell.

238
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:09,380
Now, you haven't been doing anything foolish?

239
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:15,930
I do hope you've forgotten all about that silly little plan of yours.

240
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:19,600
- Which one? - About blowing up the White House.

241
00:16:19,890 --> 00:16:23,230
Oh, Ma, I was only fooling.

242
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:25,810
Besides, what would the president say?

243
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:30,610
You're a naughty boy, Bruno.

244
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:34,280
You could always make me laugh.

245
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:42,290
Get shaved before your father gets home.

246
00:16:44,830 --> 00:16:48,960
I'm sick and tired of bowing and scraping to the king.

247
00:16:49,630 --> 00:16:52,460
Now, now. Now, let's not lose control.

248
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:54,420
Come see my painting.

249
00:16:56,970 --> 00:17:00,100
Bruno, I do wish you'd take up painting.

250
00:17:00,350 --> 00:17:02,770
Such a soothing pastime.

251
00:17:20,990 --> 00:17:23,660
Oh, Mother, you're wonderful!

252
00:17:23,910 --> 00:17:27,170
That's the old boy, all right. That's Father.

253
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:33,170
Is it?

254
00:17:36,590 --> 00:17:39,300
I was trying to paint Saint Francis.

255
00:17:39,510 --> 00:17:43,680
Excuse me, sir. They're ready with your call to Southampton.

256
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,690
I want to talk to you and your mother.

257
00:17:54,690 --> 00:17:56,990
Sorry, Father. Long distance.

258
00:17:58,780 --> 00:18:01,780
- Must you take that tone with Bruno? - Hello.

259
00:18:05,710 --> 00:18:08,210
Guy. Bruno.

260
00:18:08,670 --> 00:18:11,330
What? Now, who did you say this is?

261
00:18:11,630 --> 00:18:16,380
Bruno, Guy. Bruno Anthony. Don't you remember? On the train.

262
00:18:18,010 --> 00:18:20,470
Are you getting your divorce?

263
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:24,720
He should be sent someplace for treatment before it's too late!

264
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:27,060
Oh, so she double-crossed you.

265
00:18:27,230 --> 00:18:28,640
Are you going to see her again?

266
00:18:31,900 --> 00:18:33,900
Now, just let's have lunch first...

267
00:18:34,070 --> 00:18:37,210
I'm going to have that boy taken care of.

268
00:18:37,210 --> 00:18:39,240
If necessary, put under restraint!

269
00:19:41,720 --> 00:19:44,260
- Good night, Mom. - Now, don't stay out late.

270
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:46,930
- There's the bus! - Hold on, driver!

271
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:48,970
- Hey, driver! - Hey!

272
00:19:49,430 --> 00:19:51,060
- Hurry! - Hey, wait a minute!

273
00:19:51,230 --> 00:19:52,810
Driver.

274
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:58,820
- I'll get it, Tom. Go ahead. - Okay.

275
00:20:17,210 --> 00:20:18,500
- Watch your step. - Thanks.

276
00:20:18,670 --> 00:20:19,960
Easy does it, Tommy.

277
00:20:20,130 --> 00:20:23,170
- Hey, I'm hungry. - Oh, hungry already!

278
00:20:29,430 --> 00:20:34,190
Hot dogs! Get them while they're hot! Red-hot! Right over here!

279
00:20:34,350 --> 00:20:36,920
Yes, sir, get your popcorn here.

280
00:20:36,920 --> 00:20:39,150
Fresh popcorn! Only 5 cents a bag!

281
00:20:39,360 --> 00:20:40,650
Come and get it!

282
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:46,700
Come and get it! Here you are!

283
00:20:47,490 --> 00:20:49,870
- Three cones, Arthur. - What flavors you got?

284
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:52,080
- Vanilla. - Vanilla? Swell.

285
00:20:54,830 --> 00:20:57,630
- I should've had a hot dog before this. - A hot dog?

286
00:20:57,840 --> 00:20:59,710
Satisfy my craving a little better.

287
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:00,880
Craving for what?

288
00:21:00,880 --> 00:21:02,880
I never saw a girl eat so much.

289
00:21:05,510 --> 00:21:07,550
- Where do you put it all? - This is good.

290
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:08,970
Want some money?

291
00:21:14,890 --> 00:21:16,480
Are we going to the Tunnel of Love?

292
00:21:16,650 --> 00:21:19,110
- The Tunnel of Love? Come on! - Yeah. Great. Come on.

293
00:21:22,900 --> 00:21:24,860
- What do you call that? - I don't know.

294
00:21:25,030 --> 00:21:27,030
You think we should get her weighed?

295
00:21:30,370 --> 00:21:34,000
Stick them up. Bang, bang.

296
00:21:36,540 --> 00:21:40,130
Come closer, come closer! Step right up and win a baby doll.

297
00:21:40,590 --> 00:21:42,710
Let's go see who's the strongest.

298
00:21:46,430 --> 00:21:50,180
Sixteen hundred. Who wants to take a chance to win a Kewpie Doll?

299
00:21:50,350 --> 00:21:52,720
- Strongest pays for the merry-go-round. - Fair enough.

300
00:21:52,930 --> 00:21:54,770
Here we go. Watch it.

301
00:21:54,930 --> 00:21:56,390
Come on.

302
00:21:58,230 --> 00:22:01,020
Sixteen hundred. How about you, sir?

303
00:22:01,270 --> 00:22:02,520
Thank you.

304
00:22:02,730 --> 00:22:04,280
Here we go!

305
00:22:05,950 --> 00:22:09,280
Seventeen hundred. You did not win the Kewpie Doll that time.

306
00:22:09,570 --> 00:22:11,620
Will you try it again, sir?

307
00:22:12,870 --> 00:22:15,370
Here we go. Watch it now.

308
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:18,390
Sixteen hundred. Will you try it again

309
00:22:18,390 --> 00:22:20,920
and win a nice Kewpie Doll for the lady?

310
00:22:21,170 --> 00:22:22,670
Try your luck, mister?

311
00:22:24,550 --> 00:22:26,310
Come closer, come closer!

312
00:22:26,310 --> 00:22:28,340
Step right up and win a baby doll!

313
00:22:28,510 --> 00:22:30,300
Hot dogs! Here you are, folks!

314
00:22:30,470 --> 00:22:33,720
Get them while they're hot! Red-hot! Right over here!

315
00:22:34,100 --> 00:22:35,600
Here we go.

316
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:45,400
You just won a Kewpie Doll.

317
00:22:45,610 --> 00:22:47,900
Why, he's broken the thing.

318
00:22:49,110 --> 00:22:51,240
Come on, Miriam. Come on.

319
00:22:51,410 --> 00:22:54,240
Everybody, step right up and get your ticket cashed!

320
00:22:54,410 --> 00:22:56,450
Let's go on the merry-go-round!

321
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:32,950
Let's sing. Come on.

322
00:24:00,390 --> 00:24:02,350
Let's go for a boat ride.

323
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:07,860
Get your popcorn here! Fresh popcorn!

324
00:24:08,900 --> 00:24:11,490
- How about some popcorn? - You don't want any more to eat.

325
00:24:11,650 --> 00:24:12,950
Not now!

326
00:24:13,110 --> 00:24:15,280
No fun necking with a mouthful of popcorn.

327
00:24:15,450 --> 00:24:18,200
- Come on. - Okay.

328
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:24,370
Take a ride. Take a slide.

329
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:29,750
Take a trip with lots of zip. This way, ladies and gentlemen.

330
00:24:34,010 --> 00:24:36,430
- How about this one? - I'm gonna drive.

331
00:24:36,680 --> 00:24:38,600
You're gonna drive?

332
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:41,890
Don't rock the boat!

333
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:45,850
- Here we go. - Anchors aweigh, admiral.

334
00:24:46,060 --> 00:24:48,230
You sure you know how to steer this tub?

335
00:25:23,640 --> 00:25:27,650
- Make him stop! Make him stop! - I'm trying to steer the boat.

336
00:25:28,900 --> 00:25:31,400
Hey, what's that? What are you doing?

337
00:25:32,230 --> 00:25:34,490
Come on, come on, Miriam.

338
00:25:41,660 --> 00:25:43,660
- Come on. - Stop it!

339
00:25:57,260 --> 00:25:59,010
Hang on.

340
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:07,520
- Oh, wait a minute. - Easy does it, now. Let's not break a leg.

341
00:26:07,770 --> 00:26:09,770
We got use for you later.

342
00:26:27,790 --> 00:26:29,370
Hey, Miriam!

343
00:26:36,220 --> 00:26:37,630
Is your name Miriam?

344
00:26:37,880 --> 00:26:40,430
Why, yes. How did you...?

345
00:27:02,950 --> 00:27:04,580
Hey, Miriam!

346
00:27:05,740 --> 00:27:07,750
Miriam, where are you?

347
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:09,540
Miriam!

348
00:27:09,710 --> 00:27:12,640
Miriam, come on. Stop fooling.

349
00:27:12,640 --> 00:27:13,880
Think you can give us the slip?

350
00:27:14,090 --> 00:27:17,170
- Hey, come on! Stop hiding. - Miriam, where are you?

351
00:27:21,590 --> 00:27:24,430
Lost your girl, George? Miriam!

352
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:26,260
Hey, here she is.

353
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:31,730
Look, she's fainted.

354
00:27:32,600 --> 00:27:34,860
Come on, Miriam, get up.

355
00:27:36,110 --> 00:27:37,860
What's the matter?

356
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:42,280
- She's dead! - Dead?

357
00:27:42,530 --> 00:27:43,950
Help, somebody, help!

358
00:27:44,200 --> 00:27:46,240
Somebody send for a doctor!

359
00:27:46,490 --> 00:27:48,370
Help!

360
00:27:48,790 --> 00:27:51,750
Help! Help!

361
00:27:53,500 --> 00:27:55,710
Help, somebody, help!

362
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:59,380
Help! Bring help! Somebody bring a doctor! Help!

363
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:13,900
Go get a cop. I don't know what's going on over there.

364
00:28:35,420 --> 00:28:36,880
Thank you.

365
00:29:01,110 --> 00:29:04,360
- What's your opinion? - You'll never make the Metropolitan.

366
00:29:06,200 --> 00:29:07,660
Name's Collins.

367
00:29:07,870 --> 00:29:11,370
On sabbatical, Delaware Tech. Glad to meet you.

368
00:29:13,540 --> 00:29:17,880
I just made a speech in New York on integration.

369
00:29:18,790 --> 00:29:23,010
In differential calculus, a function is given...

370
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:27,140
and the differential is obtained. You understand?

371
00:29:27,470 --> 00:29:29,470
Yes, I understand.

372
00:29:29,970 --> 00:29:31,640
You do?

373
00:30:05,720 --> 00:30:07,510
Guy!

374
00:30:13,020 --> 00:30:14,930
Guy.

375
00:30:16,690 --> 00:30:18,020
Over here, Guy.

376
00:30:45,670 --> 00:30:48,260
- Hello, Guy. - What are you doing here?

377
00:30:48,470 --> 00:30:49,760
At this time of night?

378
00:30:49,930 --> 00:30:52,390
You don't seem very pleased to see me.

379
00:30:52,640 --> 00:30:55,180
- I've brought you a little present. - What do you mean?

380
00:30:58,810 --> 00:31:00,600
What's this all about?

381
00:31:00,810 --> 00:31:02,560
Recognize them?

382
00:31:06,780 --> 00:31:08,070
It was very quick.

383
00:31:08,780 --> 00:31:10,490
She wasn't hurt in any way.

384
00:31:10,700 --> 00:31:12,990
It was all over in no time.

385
00:31:13,660 --> 00:31:15,910
I knew you'd be surprised.

386
00:31:16,950 --> 00:31:20,250
There's nothing for us to worry about. Nobody saw me.

387
00:31:21,500 --> 00:31:23,210
Only Miriam.

388
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:25,960
And I was very careful, Guy.

389
00:31:26,170 --> 00:31:30,170
Even when I dropped your cigarette lighter there, I went back to pick it up.

390
00:31:32,680 --> 00:31:36,350
Are you trying to tell me...? Why, you maniac!

391
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:39,120
But, Guy, you wanted it.

392
00:31:39,120 --> 00:31:41,770
We planned it on the train together, remember?

393
00:31:41,980 --> 00:31:44,770
- Where are you going? - Where do you think I'm going?

394
00:31:44,980 --> 00:31:48,030
- I'm gonna call the police, of course. - But you can't.

395
00:31:48,280 --> 00:31:50,150
We'd both be arrested for murder.

396
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:52,770
We'd both be arrested for murder?

397
00:31:52,770 --> 00:31:54,620
You're just as much in it as I am.

398
00:31:54,990 --> 00:31:57,540
We planned it together. Crisscross.

399
00:31:58,900 --> 00:32:00,830
You think you can get away with it?

400
00:32:01,040 --> 00:32:02,130
Oh, come, now.

401
00:32:02,130 --> 00:32:05,130
Why should I go to Metcalf to kill a total stranger?

402
00:32:05,380 --> 00:32:08,380
Unless it was part of the plan, and you were in on it.

403
00:32:09,050 --> 00:32:11,380
You're the one who benefits, Guy.

404
00:32:11,680 --> 00:32:14,050
You're a free man now.

405
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:16,800
I didn't even know the girl.

406
00:32:18,890 --> 00:32:22,560
I had nothing to do with this. The police will believe me.

407
00:32:22,810 --> 00:32:24,900
Guy, if you go to the police now...

408
00:32:25,150 --> 00:32:28,270
you'll just be turning yourself in as an accessory.

409
00:32:28,530 --> 00:32:31,820
You see, you have the motive.

410
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:41,000
- What is it? - My telephone.

411
00:32:44,370 --> 00:32:47,250
Someone has some news for you, Guy.

412
00:32:52,340 --> 00:32:54,090
It's the police.

413
00:33:21,040 --> 00:33:23,450
You've got me acting like I'm a criminal.

414
00:33:24,620 --> 00:33:27,420
Why, you crazy fool!

415
00:33:29,340 --> 00:33:31,460
Don't you call me that.

416
00:33:34,130 --> 00:33:38,760
Oh. Oh, you must be tired, Guy. I know I am.

417
00:33:38,970 --> 00:33:41,470
I've had a strenuous evening.

418
00:33:41,970 --> 00:33:46,890
But now, about my father, I've got the plan of the house already made.

419
00:33:47,100 --> 00:33:51,070
And I have an old Luger pistol I got in a pawnshop in San Francisco...

420
00:33:51,480 --> 00:33:53,420
But, Guy... Guy, wait a minute!

421
00:33:53,420 --> 00:33:55,650
We have to talk. We have to arrange things.

422
00:33:55,900 --> 00:33:58,160
Go, before I give you what you gave Miriam.

423
00:33:58,370 --> 00:34:00,870
You're not yourself, Guy. You're tired.

424
00:34:01,080 --> 00:34:04,750
When you think things over, you'll see I'm right. Tomorrow, we...

425
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:08,460
I never saw you before! I never want to see you again!

426
00:34:08,670 --> 00:34:10,000
But we have...

427
00:34:22,180 --> 00:34:24,850
Yes? Yes, Anne.

428
00:34:25,430 --> 00:34:27,770
I'm sorry, darling. I just got in.

429
00:34:28,480 --> 00:34:31,690
Of course I'm all right, but you sound upset.

430
00:34:31,940 --> 00:34:33,610
Is anything wrong?

431
00:34:35,360 --> 00:34:37,780
All right. I'll come over right away.

432
00:35:00,050 --> 00:35:02,550
Anne, darling, you're trembling.

433
00:35:08,390 --> 00:35:12,810
Guy, I wonder if you know how much I love you.

434
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:16,150
Brazen woman, I'm the one to say that.

435
00:35:24,990 --> 00:35:27,200
I wanted you to know before...

436
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:30,960
Father wants to see you.

437
00:35:48,980 --> 00:35:51,190
Good evening, sir.

438
00:35:56,070 --> 00:35:57,940
Hello, Babs.

439
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:03,910
- Something dreadful's happened. - Sit down, Barbara.

440
00:36:07,950 --> 00:36:12,870
There seems to be no way of diplomatically breaking tragic news.

441
00:36:13,080 --> 00:36:16,210
I'm sorry, Guy, to be the one to tell you.

442
00:36:16,420 --> 00:36:18,500
It concerns your wife.

443
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:22,130
She's been murdered.

444
00:36:23,130 --> 00:36:25,300
The police have been trying to find you.

445
00:36:25,550 --> 00:36:28,100
You're to call headquarters in Metcalf.

446
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:33,270
Miriam murdered?

447
00:36:34,100 --> 00:36:35,810
She was strangled.

448
00:36:39,650 --> 00:36:42,530
It happened on an island, in an amusement park.

449
00:36:42,740 --> 00:36:45,240
It was sort of a lovers' lane, I believe.

450
00:36:45,450 --> 00:36:47,160
Evidently, a sordid atmosphere.

451
00:36:47,410 --> 00:36:50,010
She went there with the boys that found her,

452
00:36:50,010 --> 00:36:51,080
so they're not suspects.

453
00:36:51,250 --> 00:36:52,580
But you will be.

454
00:36:52,830 --> 00:36:55,830
We can't overlook that murder is at our doorstep...

455
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:57,590
but don't drag it in here.

456
00:36:57,840 --> 00:36:59,420
Let's not fool ourselves.

457
00:36:59,590 --> 00:37:02,880
Police will say Guy wanted her dead so he could marry Anne.

458
00:37:03,090 --> 00:37:06,010
This sort of crime, they go after the husband.

459
00:37:06,260 --> 00:37:07,550
Guy had every motive.

460
00:37:08,430 --> 00:37:10,260
Motive?

461
00:37:10,600 --> 00:37:12,180
Well, she's right.

462
00:37:12,430 --> 00:37:15,600
Whichever way you look at it, I'm in a spot.

463
00:37:15,850 --> 00:37:18,440
I'm sure you've got nothing to worry about.

464
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:22,400
If he hasn't an alibi for 9:30, he has plenty to worry about.

465
00:37:22,610 --> 00:37:25,490
- You can tell them where you were. - Sherry.

466
00:37:25,700 --> 00:37:27,200
Thank you.

467
00:37:27,370 --> 00:37:29,370
Can't you, Guy?

468
00:37:29,530 --> 00:37:32,750
Nine-thirty? I was on the train from New York to Washington.

469
00:37:32,910 --> 00:37:34,200
There you are.

470
00:37:34,410 --> 00:37:37,750
Who saw you? Did you speak to anyone? You'll need a witness.

471
00:37:37,960 --> 00:37:40,090
Yes, I did speak to someone.

472
00:37:40,300 --> 00:37:43,010
- Someone you know? - No.

473
00:37:43,260 --> 00:37:44,880
His name was...

474
00:37:45,300 --> 00:37:48,800
C... Col... Collins! He's a professor.

475
00:37:49,890 --> 00:37:52,810
- Harvard? - Delaware Tech.

476
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:56,520
Then everything's all right.

477
00:37:56,730 --> 00:37:59,810
Not quite. He'll still have to answer questions.

478
00:38:00,020 --> 00:38:01,400
Routine. Pure routine.

479
00:38:01,650 --> 00:38:04,400
There'll be many reporters here in the morning.

480
00:38:04,610 --> 00:38:08,320
Daddy doesn't mind a little scandal. He's a senator.

481
00:38:10,330 --> 00:38:13,290
It can't be helped, darling. It's not your fault.

482
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:16,080
No one can say you had anything to do with it.

483
00:38:18,670 --> 00:38:23,670
Someone might say it. I'd do anything to keep you all out of this mess.

484
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:27,170
Be guided by my experience.

485
00:38:27,430 --> 00:38:31,550
Never lose any sleep over accusations. Unless they can be proved, of course.

486
00:38:32,310 --> 00:38:35,810
Dreadful business. Dreadful. Poor, unfortunate girl.

487
00:38:36,060 --> 00:38:37,520
She was a tramp.

488
00:38:37,690 --> 00:38:39,020
She was a human being.

489
00:38:39,190 --> 00:38:43,360
Even the most unworthy of us has a right to life and the pursuit of happiness.

490
00:38:43,650 --> 00:38:46,360
From what I hear, she pursued it in all directions.

491
00:38:49,570 --> 00:38:53,280
Father, it's getting terribly late. Guy looks so tired.

492
00:38:53,540 --> 00:38:56,200
Of course, of course. Back to bed, Barbara.

493
00:38:56,450 --> 00:39:00,330
Nothing stands in your way. Now you can be married right away.

494
00:39:00,540 --> 00:39:02,130
Think of it, you're free!

495
00:39:02,290 --> 00:39:04,590
You don't always have to say what you think.

496
00:39:04,800 --> 00:39:06,460
Father, I am not a politician.

497
00:39:06,630 --> 00:39:08,510
Remember to call Captain Turley.

498
00:39:08,760 --> 00:39:10,760
Yes, sir. Good night.

499
00:39:12,720 --> 00:39:14,180
- Anne... - It'd be wonderful...

500
00:39:14,350 --> 00:39:17,430
to have a man love you so much he'd kill for you.

501
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:22,900
I kept saying over and over I was being silly.

502
00:39:23,150 --> 00:39:27,440
But there was one horrible moment tonight when the news came through.

503
00:39:27,650 --> 00:39:31,450
I kept remembering what you shouted on the phone from Metcalf.

504
00:39:31,700 --> 00:39:33,070
That I could...

505
00:39:34,830 --> 00:39:36,240
Don't even say it.

506
00:39:36,490 --> 00:39:38,500
Forget you ever said it.

507
00:39:38,750 --> 00:39:41,420
Even more terrifying than the murder itself was...

508
00:39:41,670 --> 00:39:45,000
the horrible thought that if you had done it...

509
00:39:45,250 --> 00:39:48,960
we would have been separated. Perhaps forever.

510
00:39:49,220 --> 00:39:51,010
I couldn't stand that.

511
00:39:51,260 --> 00:39:52,890
I couldn't bear it.

512
00:40:06,190 --> 00:40:09,150
Captain Turley is expecting me. Guy Haines.

513
00:40:09,400 --> 00:40:11,450
Just a moment, Mr. Haines.

514
00:40:21,960 --> 00:40:24,210
Would you come in, please?

515
00:40:29,260 --> 00:40:31,630
Good of you to be so prompt, Mr. Haines.

516
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:33,840
- This is Lieutenant Campbell. - How do you do?

517
00:40:34,010 --> 00:40:36,140
- Won't you sit down? - Thank you, sir.

518
00:40:36,350 --> 00:40:38,130
I know you're busy,

519
00:40:38,130 --> 00:40:40,640
so we won't detain you any longer than necessary.

520
00:40:40,850 --> 00:40:45,190
You've already been good enough to tell us where you were last evening.

521
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:48,150
We've found the gentleman you spoke with on the train.

522
00:40:48,400 --> 00:40:51,030
- I saw him outside. - Please come in, professor.

523
00:40:57,120 --> 00:40:59,490
Professor Collins, this is Mr. Haines.

524
00:40:59,700 --> 00:41:02,660
He was with you on the train last night.

525
00:41:03,830 --> 00:41:07,380
I'm sorry, but I really don't remember meeting this gentleman.

526
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:12,970
Unfortunately, I remember very little of our trip from New York.

527
00:41:13,170 --> 00:41:16,390
You see, we had a little celebration.

528
00:41:16,550 --> 00:41:19,470
But we were sitting opposite each other...

529
00:41:19,720 --> 00:41:21,680
in the observation car.

530
00:41:21,890 --> 00:41:25,190
- You were singing a song about a goat. - A goat?

531
00:41:25,350 --> 00:41:28,060
And calculus. You were going over a speech you made.

532
00:41:28,480 --> 00:41:30,070
I was?

533
00:41:30,980 --> 00:41:33,240
I'm very sorry, Mr. Haines.

534
00:41:33,490 --> 00:41:35,900
I certainly must have been celebrating.

535
00:41:39,910 --> 00:41:44,290
Captain, is it so important whether Professor Collins remembers me?

536
00:41:44,500 --> 00:41:48,000
I've been able to name a man who was on the train with me.

537
00:41:48,250 --> 00:41:49,670
You've found him.

538
00:41:49,880 --> 00:41:53,210
Isn't that proof of where I was at 9:30 last night?

539
00:41:55,760 --> 00:41:58,180
Thank you. Hello, dear. Senator. Babs.

540
00:41:58,390 --> 00:42:01,260
- Hello, darling. Have you had dinner? - On the train.

541
00:42:01,510 --> 00:42:04,980
You've been in Metcalf all this time? We expected you hours ago.

542
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:09,270
I didn't. They sometimes leave a suspect in the can all night.

543
00:42:09,520 --> 00:42:12,400
Sit down, Guy. Sit down. Barbara, some coffee.

544
00:42:12,860 --> 00:42:15,240
You had no trouble with the police...

545
00:42:15,490 --> 00:42:17,610
once they verified your alibi?

546
00:42:17,860 --> 00:42:20,780
When an alibi is full of bourbon, it can't stand up.

547
00:42:21,030 --> 00:42:23,490
You mean the professor was boiled?

548
00:42:23,750 --> 00:42:25,040
Completely.

549
00:42:25,290 --> 00:42:26,580
He didn't remember me.

550
00:42:27,790 --> 00:42:31,670
But you knew he was on the train. Isn't that proof you were on it too?

551
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:34,130
Apparently, not at the right time.

552
00:42:34,380 --> 00:42:37,760
They suggested I could have caught the train at Baltimore...

553
00:42:38,010 --> 00:42:40,140
after Miriam was murdered.

554
00:42:41,050 --> 00:42:43,600
They had it all worked out in their timetable.

555
00:42:43,810 --> 00:42:47,980
Well, that's ridiculous. They're acting as if you were guilty.

556
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:51,310
Everything will be all right.

557
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:54,110
The police were just being thorough.

558
00:42:54,780 --> 00:42:56,070
Weren't they, Daddy?

559
00:42:57,320 --> 00:43:00,200
I certainly hope so. What's your next move?

560
00:43:02,330 --> 00:43:05,160
Whatever it is, the police will know about it.

561
00:43:05,370 --> 00:43:08,500
Oh. Oh, they gave me a present.

562
00:43:10,330 --> 00:43:11,630
Take a look.

563
00:43:14,590 --> 00:43:16,420
My guardian angel.

564
00:43:16,710 --> 00:43:18,340
You're being tailed.

565
00:43:18,720 --> 00:43:21,010
That's Leslie Hennessy.

566
00:43:21,260 --> 00:43:22,720
He works 16 hours a day.

567
00:43:22,970 --> 00:43:25,390
Somebody else takes over for the next eight.

568
00:43:25,600 --> 00:43:27,680
Actually, he's a very nice fellow.

569
00:43:27,890 --> 00:43:29,560
I'll have him called off.

570
00:43:29,810 --> 00:43:32,400
Obstructing the wheels ofjustice, Daddy?

571
00:43:33,360 --> 00:43:35,770
I'm afraid where I go, Hennessy goes.

572
00:43:35,980 --> 00:43:37,690
Even to the Senate.

573
00:43:38,990 --> 00:43:42,610
- Is he likely to picket my office? - Very likely.

574
00:43:45,490 --> 00:43:48,870
I would suggest, for your own peace of mind, of course...

575
00:43:49,120 --> 00:43:53,080
that you work at the house. It'll be less embarrassing for you.

576
00:43:53,290 --> 00:43:55,230
But what about practicing?

577
00:43:55,230 --> 00:43:57,710
Perhaps I'd better forget about Forest Hills.

578
00:43:58,920 --> 00:44:00,260
My dear boy...

579
00:44:00,510 --> 00:44:05,260
wouldn't it look rather awkward if you suddenly canceled all your plans?

580
00:44:05,510 --> 00:44:08,810
He's right. You mustn't do anything that looks suspicious.

581
00:44:09,060 --> 00:44:13,980
- You must act as if nothing happened. - Escorted by Mr. Hennessy.

582
00:44:16,650 --> 00:44:19,650
A call for you, Mr. Haines. They say it's urgent.

583
00:44:19,900 --> 00:44:21,280
Thank you.

584
00:44:22,360 --> 00:44:23,700
Excuse me.

585
00:44:23,950 --> 00:44:26,240
- Hello? - Hello, Guy.

586
00:44:29,410 --> 00:44:32,960
It must have been some mistake. It wasn't for me.

587
00:44:41,130 --> 00:44:43,430
I suppose I was lucky being seeded fifth.

588
00:44:43,680 --> 00:44:48,600
I've never seen the Forest Hills tournament. I look forward to it.

589
00:44:48,810 --> 00:44:51,180
You mean we'll be going together, Hennessy?

590
00:44:51,350 --> 00:44:55,690
Ah, don't worry. By that time, this thing will be all cleared up.

591
00:44:56,770 --> 00:45:00,280
- You ever think of turning professional? - I don't have to do that.

592
00:45:00,490 --> 00:45:02,740
After tennis, I'm going into politics.

593
00:45:03,820 --> 00:45:06,530
- I hope. - Politics?

594
00:45:06,820 --> 00:45:09,280
It's good I don't report that to the chief.

595
00:45:09,540 --> 00:45:11,880
If he knew that,

596
00:45:11,880 --> 00:45:13,120
he'd put 10 men on you. He says...

597
00:45:13,370 --> 00:45:15,620
Let's take this cab. It's getting late.

598
00:45:17,380 --> 00:45:19,960
- Pentagon building, please. - Oh, no, not there.

599
00:45:20,130 --> 00:45:22,170
I always get lost.

600
00:46:28,030 --> 00:46:29,740
We'd better be getting back.

601
00:46:29,950 --> 00:46:34,580
We've actually been alone for an hour. Seems almost indecent.

602
00:46:34,830 --> 00:46:36,120
You like?

603
00:46:36,290 --> 00:46:37,580
I like.

604
00:46:37,750 --> 00:46:40,460
I was beginning to feel like a goldfish.

605
00:46:40,630 --> 00:46:42,420
Guy!

606
00:46:46,720 --> 00:46:48,300
Excuse me.

607
00:46:54,100 --> 00:46:56,020
Will you stop pestering me?

608
00:46:56,270 --> 00:46:59,980
You're spoiling everything. You're making me come out into the open.

609
00:47:01,900 --> 00:47:04,360
I've tried calling you on the phone.

610
00:47:04,530 --> 00:47:06,400
Did you get my note?

611
00:47:06,780 --> 00:47:08,210
Why haven't you called me?

612
00:47:08,210 --> 00:47:10,660
My father is leaving for Florida next week.

613
00:47:10,870 --> 00:47:15,120
Listen, you... There's a detective outside. He'll see us together.

614
00:47:17,330 --> 00:47:19,660
Isn't that Anne Morton?

615
00:47:22,460 --> 00:47:25,710
Slight improvement over Miriam, huh, Guy?

616
00:47:27,050 --> 00:47:29,670
Stay away from me, I'm telling you.

617
00:47:34,560 --> 00:47:35,850
Who is it, Guy?

618
00:47:36,020 --> 00:47:39,140
I never saw him before. Just some tennis fan.

619
00:47:45,900 --> 00:47:48,650
Here's a special delivery. It's marked "personal."

620
00:47:48,900 --> 00:47:51,700
- Thank you. - You getting any practice today?

621
00:47:51,910 --> 00:47:54,160
Yes, if I can get a court at the club.

622
00:47:56,490 --> 00:47:59,450
- Barbara, who are you waving at? - Mr. Hennessy.

623
00:47:59,870 --> 00:48:02,370
It's a shame Daddy won't let us have him in.

624
00:48:04,420 --> 00:48:06,090
- You met him yet, Louise? - No.

625
00:48:06,250 --> 00:48:07,710
He's awfully cute.

626
00:48:58,890 --> 00:49:00,680
Okay, you ready, Guy?

627
00:49:02,390 --> 00:49:03,810
Yeah.

628
00:49:21,540 --> 00:49:26,170
Hi, darling. This is Mr. Anthony, a friend of Monsieur and Madame Darville.

629
00:49:26,420 --> 00:49:27,670
Guy Haines.

630
00:49:27,840 --> 00:49:30,710
I've been a fan for a long time, Mr. Haines.

631
00:49:30,920 --> 00:49:32,670
I follow everything you do.

632
00:49:32,880 --> 00:49:37,590
Mr. Anthony has been telling us some charming stories. Very funny.

633
00:50:03,700 --> 00:50:05,500
I've been talking to your shadow.

634
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:08,120
Guy, did you know Mr. Hennessy helped crack

635
00:50:08,120 --> 00:50:08,710
that ax murder?

636
00:50:09,000 --> 00:50:12,250
The one with the body cut up and hidden in the butcher shop?

637
00:50:12,420 --> 00:50:15,550
He was locked in the icebox with the left leg for six hours.

638
00:50:15,800 --> 00:50:18,130
He pulls those things out of his hat.

639
00:50:18,340 --> 00:50:21,300
Who's the interesting Frenchman with the Darvilles?

640
00:50:22,890 --> 00:50:24,600
His name's Anthony.

641
00:50:24,980 --> 00:50:26,690
He's not French.

642
00:50:30,020 --> 00:50:32,480
- How are you? - Delightful to see you, Barbara.

643
00:50:32,650 --> 00:50:34,070
How sweet you look.

644
00:50:34,320 --> 00:50:36,820
I hope you won't forget our party on Thursday, madame.

645
00:50:36,990 --> 00:50:39,660
- We're planning on it. - But of course.

646
00:50:39,870 --> 00:50:42,740
This is my sister. Barbara, this is Mr. Anthony.

647
00:50:42,950 --> 00:50:44,990
How do you do?

648
00:50:51,540 --> 00:50:53,380
Is your name Miriam?

649
00:51:31,420 --> 00:51:32,790
- Hi, Hennessy. - Hi.

650
00:51:33,040 --> 00:51:35,210
- I won't keep you out late tonight. - Oh?

651
00:51:35,380 --> 00:51:37,920
With Forest Hills tomorrow, I've got to sleep.

652
00:51:38,130 --> 00:51:39,670
That's too bad.

653
00:51:39,880 --> 00:51:42,430
Hammond will be on duty in a couple of hours.

654
00:51:42,680 --> 00:51:44,800
I'd like to see him earn his salary.

655
00:51:45,060 --> 00:51:47,180
Doesn't that bloodhound ever relax?

656
00:51:47,390 --> 00:51:50,140
He sticks so close, he's beginning to grow on me.

657
00:51:50,390 --> 00:51:51,810
Like a fungus.

658
00:51:52,060 --> 00:51:54,520
He thinks you're a very suspicious character.

659
00:51:54,770 --> 00:51:58,650
But then he doesn't trust anybody, not even himself.

660
00:52:01,530 --> 00:52:02,820
Come on, Hennessy.

661
00:52:02,990 --> 00:52:05,120
- Don't forget your sleeping bag. - Yeah.

662
00:52:05,410 --> 00:52:08,450
If I wait too long on a sidewalk, my feet get cold.

663
00:52:08,700 --> 00:52:12,120
- If I sit too long on those stone steps... - Don't worry.

664
00:52:12,330 --> 00:52:13,250
Since you

665
00:52:13,250 --> 00:52:16,170
told Barbara about that icebox, you're her favorite charity.

666
00:52:16,420 --> 00:52:19,710
- She'll send a butler to defrost you. - Cute kid.

667
00:53:05,470 --> 00:53:07,100
Good evening, Miss Morton.

668
00:53:07,350 --> 00:53:10,010
- This is Mr. Anthony, Father. - Mr. Anthony.

669
00:53:10,600 --> 00:53:12,060
How do you do, sir?

670
00:53:12,270 --> 00:53:14,600
I'd like to talk with you sometime, sir...

671
00:53:14,850 --> 00:53:18,770
and tell you about my idea for harnessing a life force.

672
00:53:19,020 --> 00:53:22,900
It'll make atomic power look like the horse and buggy.

673
00:53:23,150 --> 00:53:27,410
I'm already developing my faculty for seeing millions of miles.

674
00:53:27,660 --> 00:53:29,610
And, senator,

675
00:53:29,610 --> 00:53:34,000
can you imagine being able to smell a flower on the planet Mars?

676
00:53:34,870 --> 00:53:37,250
I'd like to have lunch with you soon, sir...

677
00:53:37,420 --> 00:53:38,730
tell you more about it.

678
00:53:39,210 --> 00:53:41,250
- I'll see you later. - Goodbye.

679
00:53:45,890 --> 00:53:48,390
I don't remember inviting him. Who is he?

680
00:53:48,640 --> 00:53:50,640
A friend of the Darvilles.

681
00:53:51,680 --> 00:53:53,640
Unusual personality.

682
00:53:57,900 --> 00:54:00,650
- Excuse me, aren't you Judge Donahue? - That's right.

683
00:54:00,860 --> 00:54:03,440
How do you do, sir? My name is Bruno Anthony.

684
00:54:03,860 --> 00:54:05,650
What's he doing here?

685
00:54:05,860 --> 00:54:07,740
I don't know.

686
00:54:10,530 --> 00:54:13,540
After you've sentenced a man to the chair...

687
00:54:13,750 --> 00:54:17,330
isn't it difficult to go out and eat your dinner after that?

688
00:54:20,710 --> 00:54:24,340
When a murderer's caught, he's tried. When convicted, he's sentenced.

689
00:54:24,590 --> 00:54:27,380
When he's sentenced to death, he must be executed.

690
00:54:29,010 --> 00:54:31,430
- Quite impersonal, isn't it? - So it is.

691
00:54:31,680 --> 00:54:34,350
Besides, it doesn't happen every day.

692
00:54:34,600 --> 00:54:36,770
So few murderers are caught.

693
00:54:37,060 --> 00:54:40,730
Mr. Anthony, you seem very interested in the subject of murder.

694
00:54:40,980 --> 00:54:42,480
No more than anyone else.

695
00:54:42,730 --> 00:54:45,280
- No more than you, for instance. - Me? Oh.

696
00:54:45,740 --> 00:54:47,570
I'm not interested in murder.

697
00:54:47,780 --> 00:54:51,320
Oh, come now. Everyone is interested in that.

698
00:54:51,580 --> 00:54:54,790
Everyone has somebody that they want to put out of the way.

699
00:54:55,080 --> 00:54:57,710
Surely, madam, hasn't there been a time...

700
00:54:57,960 --> 00:55:00,580
that you didn't want to dispose of someone?

701
00:55:00,790 --> 00:55:05,510
- Your husband, for instance? - Oh, good heavens, no!

702
00:55:05,920 --> 00:55:07,590
Are you sure?

703
00:55:07,880 --> 00:55:12,390
You mean there wasn't a tiny moment when you were made very angry?

704
00:55:12,600 --> 00:55:14,260
What did you say?

705
00:55:15,100 --> 00:55:19,690
Aha. There you are, you see? There you are.

706
00:55:20,190 --> 00:55:21,940
Now...

707
00:55:22,110 --> 00:55:23,860
you're going to do a murder.

708
00:55:24,980 --> 00:55:28,110
How are you going to do it? That's the fascinating part.

709
00:55:28,280 --> 00:55:30,740
How will you do it...? I didn't get your name.

710
00:55:30,950 --> 00:55:32,200
Mrs. Cunningham.

711
00:55:32,370 --> 00:55:34,740
Mrs. Cunningham, how are you going to do it?

712
00:55:34,990 --> 00:55:38,250
Well, I suppose I'll have to get a gun from somewhere.

713
00:55:38,710 --> 00:55:40,870
Oh, no, Mrs. Cunningham.

714
00:55:41,130 --> 00:55:44,380
Bang, bang, bang, all over the place? Blood everywhere?

715
00:55:44,630 --> 00:55:46,840
- How about a little poison? - Yes.

716
00:55:47,010 --> 00:55:50,970
- Oh, that's better. That's better, Mrs... - Anderson.

717
00:55:51,220 --> 00:55:53,050
That's better, Mrs. Anderson.

718
00:55:53,550 --> 00:55:56,640
But, you see, Mrs. Cunningham is in a dreadful hurry.

719
00:55:56,850 --> 00:55:58,980
Poison takes from 10 to 12 weeks...

720
00:55:59,140 --> 00:56:02,150
if poor Mr. Cunningham is to die from natural causes.

721
00:56:04,270 --> 00:56:06,230
You know, I read of a case once.

722
00:56:06,480 --> 00:56:09,150
I think it would be a wonderful idea.

723
00:56:09,320 --> 00:56:13,160
I can take him in the car and when we get to a very lonely spot...

724
00:56:13,320 --> 00:56:17,240
hit him on the head with a hammer, pour gasoline over him and the car...

725
00:56:17,450 --> 00:56:20,160
and set the whole thing ablaze.

726
00:56:21,040 --> 00:56:22,580
And walk all the way home?

727
00:56:23,330 --> 00:56:25,210
- No? - Oh, no. No, no.

728
00:56:25,920 --> 00:56:28,500
I have the best way and the best tools.

729
00:56:28,710 --> 00:56:33,180
Simple, silent and quick. The silent part being the most important.

730
00:56:33,390 --> 00:56:35,010
Let me show you what I mean.

731
00:56:35,220 --> 00:56:37,470
May I borrow your neck for a moment?

732
00:56:37,720 --> 00:56:41,640
- Well, if it's not for long. - Oh.

733
00:56:50,030 --> 00:56:54,780
When I nod my head, you try to cry out, and I'll bet you can't do it.

734
00:56:57,910 --> 00:57:00,290
Just wait for the nod of my head.

735
00:57:28,770 --> 00:57:33,900
Mr. Anthony! Help! Help! Somebody, come quick! Help!

736
00:57:35,070 --> 00:57:37,530
Somebody, come. Quick, bring some water.

737
00:57:46,330 --> 00:57:49,420
- Let's get him out of here. - Bring him into the study.

738
00:57:50,750 --> 00:57:53,170
Will you help me take her upstairs, please?

739
00:57:53,840 --> 00:57:56,130
- What happened? - He seemed to have fainted.

740
00:57:56,390 --> 00:57:58,590
- What about her? - She was frightened.

741
00:57:58,800 --> 00:58:01,100
They were playing a game of some sort.

742
00:58:08,400 --> 00:58:11,320
I thought he was weird when he arrived. Who is he?

743
00:58:11,530 --> 00:58:12,980
I hardly know him, sir.

744
00:58:13,190 --> 00:58:16,030
Get him out of here as soon as you decently can.

745
00:58:16,280 --> 00:58:18,200
It's a nice item for the gossips.

746
00:58:18,410 --> 00:58:21,280
First thing you know, they'll be talking about orgies.

747
00:58:21,490 --> 00:58:23,830
- I better get back. - Yes, sir.

748
00:58:35,380 --> 00:58:36,970
What happened?

749
00:58:37,220 --> 00:58:41,140
I was on a merry-go-round somewhere. It made me dizzy.

750
00:58:41,390 --> 00:58:45,520
You mad, crazy maniac! You ought to be locked up.

751
00:58:46,100 --> 00:58:48,640
Will you get out of here and let me alone?

752
00:58:49,980 --> 00:58:51,480
But, Guy...

753
00:58:51,650 --> 00:58:52,900
I like you.

754
00:59:01,530 --> 00:59:03,830
You shouldn't have done that, Guy.

755
00:59:04,540 --> 00:59:06,450
Come on, pull yourself together.

756
00:59:13,550 --> 00:59:15,760
Here, let me.

757
00:59:19,260 --> 00:59:20,760
Is your car here?

758
00:59:21,680 --> 00:59:23,680
Driver's outside.

759
00:59:24,060 --> 00:59:25,350
All right, come on.

760
00:59:49,250 --> 00:59:50,500
What's the matter?

761
00:59:51,170 --> 00:59:52,670
Did you see it happen?

762
00:59:52,880 --> 00:59:54,540
He looked at me.

763
00:59:55,550 --> 00:59:58,710
His hands were on her throat...

764
00:59:58,970 --> 01:00:01,300
and he was strangling me.

765
01:00:01,800 --> 01:00:03,890
How do you mean?

766
01:00:05,680 --> 01:00:08,220
He was looking at her first...

767
01:00:08,480 --> 01:00:10,390
then looked over at me.

768
01:00:12,020 --> 01:00:15,190
He went into sort of a trance.

769
01:00:17,990 --> 01:00:19,610
Oh, it was horrible.

770
01:00:24,830 --> 01:00:27,700
He thought he was murdering me.

771
01:00:31,750 --> 01:00:33,210
But why me, Anne? Why me?

772
01:00:34,920 --> 01:00:36,920
What did I have to do with it?

773
01:00:40,470 --> 01:00:42,010
Do you know where Guy is?

774
01:00:43,180 --> 01:00:45,220
He went out with that man.

775
01:01:07,830 --> 01:01:11,330
You didn't meet him for the first time the other day, did you?

776
01:01:11,580 --> 01:01:14,330
- When you introduced us at the club? - Yes.

777
01:01:14,540 --> 01:01:17,330
Did you see how he stared at Barbara?

778
01:01:17,330 --> 01:01:18,880
No, I didn't particularly.

779
01:01:19,300 --> 01:01:21,630
He stared at her the same way tonight...

780
01:01:21,800 --> 01:01:24,760
while his hands were around Mrs. Cunningham's throat.

781
01:01:26,470 --> 01:01:28,600
What did Miriam look like?

782
01:01:30,350 --> 01:01:32,350
Why ask me? You've seen her picture.

783
01:01:32,560 --> 01:01:34,480
Go on. I want you to tell me.

784
01:01:34,730 --> 01:01:38,520
- She was dark, not too tall, rather pretty. - What else?

785
01:01:38,730 --> 01:01:40,020
What else is there?

786
01:01:41,360 --> 01:01:44,030
She wore glasses, didn't she, Guy?

787
01:01:44,400 --> 01:01:45,700
Yes.

788
01:01:46,320 --> 01:01:49,030
She looked something like Barbara, didn't she?

789
01:01:53,870 --> 01:01:56,000
How did you get him to do it?

790
01:01:57,500 --> 01:01:59,670
I get him to do it?

791
01:02:00,500 --> 01:02:03,210
He killed Miriam, didn't he?

792
01:02:04,760 --> 01:02:06,550
Tell me, didn't he?

793
01:02:07,220 --> 01:02:08,510
Yes.

794
01:02:08,720 --> 01:02:10,130
He's a maniac.

795
01:02:10,130 --> 01:02:12,010
I met him on the train going to Metcalf.

796
01:02:12,270 --> 01:02:14,800
He had a crazy scheme about exchanging murders.

797
01:02:14,800 --> 01:02:16,600
I do his, he'd do mine.

798
01:02:16,850 --> 01:02:19,520
What do you mean, your murder, Guy?

799
01:02:19,730 --> 01:02:21,270
He read about me.

800
01:02:21,270 --> 01:02:22,820
He knew about Miriam, about you.

801
01:02:23,030 --> 01:02:25,490
He suggested if he got rid of Miriam for me,

802
01:02:25,490 --> 01:02:27,030
I should kill his father.

803
01:02:27,700 --> 01:02:30,030
You must've known he was talking nonsense.

804
01:02:30,200 --> 01:02:33,120
But he wasn't. I didn't give it another thought.

805
01:02:33,370 --> 01:02:37,000
And now a lunatic wants me to kill his father.

806
01:02:38,210 --> 01:02:39,500
It's too fantastic.

807
01:02:40,000 --> 01:02:42,040
Yes, isn't it?

808
01:02:42,420 --> 01:02:45,550
You mean, you've known about Miriam all this time?

809
01:02:46,050 --> 01:02:48,800
Since the first night. He gave me her glasses.

810
01:02:49,390 --> 01:02:51,260
Why didn't you call the police?

811
01:02:51,810 --> 01:02:53,890
And have them say what you did:

812
01:02:54,270 --> 01:02:57,230
"Mr. Haines, how did you get him to do it?"

813
01:02:57,560 --> 01:03:00,310
And Bruno would say we planned it together.

814
01:03:02,820 --> 01:03:05,650
Oh, Guy, what are we gonna do?

815
01:03:05,900 --> 01:03:08,450
I don't know, Anne. I don't know.

816
01:03:09,070 --> 01:03:12,160
We'd better go inside. Your friend Hennessy is watching us.

817
01:03:12,330 --> 01:03:15,790
You see, this is why I didn't want you to know anything about it.

818
01:03:16,080 --> 01:03:19,330
I wanted to protect all of you. Barbara, your father.

819
01:03:19,580 --> 01:03:22,040
Now that you know, you're acting guilty too.

820
01:03:25,260 --> 01:03:27,420
If we could only talk to Father, to...

821
01:03:27,670 --> 01:03:31,430
It's not good. I mustn't drag anyone else into this.

822
01:03:33,260 --> 01:03:35,140
Come on, let's go.

823
01:03:44,570 --> 01:03:45,860
You look worried.

824
01:03:46,030 --> 01:03:49,110
Keep on your toes. Something funny's going on.

825
01:04:11,720 --> 01:04:14,140
I've decided to do what you want.

826
01:04:14,510 --> 01:04:17,100
I'll make that little visit to your father.

827
01:04:17,970 --> 01:04:19,600
How about tonight?

828
01:04:21,390 --> 01:04:23,730
Yes, I want to get this thing over with.

829
01:04:23,980 --> 01:04:25,810
Does anyone know you've come home?

830
01:04:26,530 --> 01:04:30,190
You'd better slip out again and stay out till after daylight.

831
01:07:50,690 --> 01:07:55,020
Mr. Anthony.

832
01:07:55,530 --> 01:07:57,980
Don't be alarmed, but I must talk to you about your son,

833
01:07:57,980 --> 01:07:59,400
about Bruno.

834
01:08:03,030 --> 01:08:04,580
Yes, Mr. Haines?

835
01:08:10,920 --> 01:08:13,830
My father isn't home tonight, Mr. Haines.

836
01:08:14,040 --> 01:08:16,460
I was about to tell you that on the phone.

837
01:08:16,710 --> 01:08:19,050
But you made such a sudden decision.

838
01:08:19,260 --> 01:08:21,050
I wondered why.

839
01:08:21,340 --> 01:08:24,640
Since you sent me the key to your house, I decided to use it...

840
01:08:24,890 --> 01:08:27,560
to make a little social call on your father.

841
01:08:27,770 --> 01:08:31,270
I thought he'd be interested to know he has a lunatic son.

842
01:08:34,770 --> 01:08:39,440
I assume that you have no intention of going ahead with our arrangement?

843
01:08:39,740 --> 01:08:42,950
None whatsoever. I never had.

844
01:08:43,570 --> 01:08:45,070
I see.

845
01:08:45,450 --> 01:08:48,410
Well, then you'll have no further use for my key.

846
01:08:52,790 --> 01:08:54,420
Nor this.

847
01:08:56,290 --> 01:08:59,800
Look, Bruno. You're terribly sick.

848
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:01,640
I don't know much about these things,

849
01:09:01,640 --> 01:09:04,260
but why don't you get some treatment?

850
01:09:04,510 --> 01:09:05,930
Not only for your sake...

851
01:09:06,430 --> 01:09:08,430
but you can't go on causing destruction...

852
01:09:08,600 --> 01:09:10,470
to anyone you happen to meet.

853
01:09:14,770 --> 01:09:17,150
I don't like to be double-crossed.

854
01:09:18,440 --> 01:09:19,940
I have a murder on my conscience,

855
01:09:19,940 --> 01:09:22,190
but it's not my murder.

856
01:09:22,450 --> 01:09:24,030
It's yours.

857
01:09:24,280 --> 01:09:26,240
And since you're the one to profit by it...

858
01:09:26,410 --> 01:09:28,830
I think you should be the one to pay for it.

859
01:09:29,790 --> 01:09:32,000
Well, I guess it's no use, Bruno.

860
01:09:32,290 --> 01:09:35,120
We seem to have nothing further to discuss.

861
01:10:19,880 --> 01:10:22,250
Don't worry. I'm not going to shoot you.

862
01:10:22,510 --> 01:10:24,510
It might disturb Mother.

863
01:10:25,010 --> 01:10:26,840
I'm a very clever fellow.

864
01:10:27,050 --> 01:10:30,850
I'll think of something better than that. Much better.

865
01:10:42,690 --> 01:10:45,780
He came back at 3:25. I didn't even know he was gone...

866
01:10:46,030 --> 01:10:48,280
until his phone rang for half an hour.

867
01:10:48,530 --> 01:10:51,450
Nobody sleeps that sound, so the janitor let me in.

868
01:10:51,620 --> 01:10:53,200
- No Haines. - I wonder where he went.

869
01:10:53,410 --> 01:10:56,200
- Probably hear of another dame murdered. - Shut up.

870
01:10:56,370 --> 01:10:58,040
How about I contact Metcalf?

871
01:10:58,210 --> 01:11:00,580
I think there's cause for more questioning.

872
01:11:00,880 --> 01:11:02,880
Questioning? Let's take him in.

873
01:11:04,550 --> 01:11:08,090
how many times must I tell you that we have nothing conclusive?

874
01:11:08,300 --> 01:11:11,380
There's no evidence he was ever at the crime scene.

875
01:11:11,380 --> 01:11:12,930
Can't you get that?

876
01:11:13,220 --> 01:11:15,600
Now, just stay put till I get back.

877
01:11:21,810 --> 01:11:23,230
Miss Morton, really.

878
01:11:23,520 --> 01:11:26,740
I know Bruno's been in some very awkward scrapes...

879
01:11:26,950 --> 01:11:29,400
but nothing so ridiculous as a murder.

880
01:11:29,570 --> 01:11:33,410
Mrs. Anthony, you have to make him do something about this.

881
01:11:33,580 --> 01:11:35,580
Don't you see that one word from him...

882
01:11:35,750 --> 01:11:38,250
would get Guy out of this dreadful situation?

883
01:11:40,080 --> 01:11:44,540
I'm sure that this whole thing is just some practical joke.

884
01:11:44,750 --> 01:11:48,920
You know, Bruno, he sometimes goes a little too far.

885
01:11:50,930 --> 01:11:54,100
I really shouldn't be saying this to an outsider...

886
01:11:54,350 --> 01:11:57,810
but sometimes he's terribly irresponsible...

887
01:11:58,100 --> 01:12:01,350
and gets into all kinds of escapades.

888
01:12:01,600 --> 01:12:04,270
Don't you understand, Mrs. Anthony?

889
01:12:04,610 --> 01:12:07,650
Your son is responsible for a woman's death.

890
01:12:08,780 --> 01:12:10,110
Did Bruno tell you this?

891
01:12:10,860 --> 01:12:12,610
Well, of course not, Mrs. Anthony.

892
01:12:12,780 --> 01:12:14,120
Well, there you are.

893
01:12:14,990 --> 01:12:18,370
Well, now, Miss Morton, it's very nice of you to call...

894
01:12:18,540 --> 01:12:21,620
and if you'll excuse me, I must get back to my painting.

895
01:12:23,130 --> 01:12:25,380
Do you care for painting, Miss Morton?

896
01:12:25,630 --> 01:12:28,130
I find it very soothing.

897
01:12:30,470 --> 01:12:32,470
Come see us again sometime.

898
01:12:49,110 --> 01:12:52,030
I'm afraid that Mother wasn't very much help, was she?

899
01:12:52,320 --> 01:12:54,610
She hasn't been well for a long time.

900
01:12:54,820 --> 01:12:57,700
She's a little... How shall I say? Confused.

901
01:12:57,990 --> 01:12:59,540
Poor Mother.

902
01:13:00,870 --> 01:13:03,330
You know, I'm very upset with Guy.

903
01:13:03,620 --> 01:13:06,880
He shouldn't have sent you on an errand like this.

904
01:13:07,170 --> 01:13:09,760
Guy doesn't know I'm here, Mr. Anthony.

905
01:13:10,760 --> 01:13:13,180
He's led you up the garden path, I'm afraid.

906
01:13:16,890 --> 01:13:19,600
He must be very desperate to try to involve me.

907
01:13:19,770 --> 01:13:23,100
I've been protecting him since that conversation on the train...

908
01:13:23,270 --> 01:13:26,020
when he told me how much he hated his wife.

909
01:13:31,360 --> 01:13:35,530
He tried to get me to go back to the island one night after dark...

910
01:13:35,740 --> 01:13:39,030
to pick up his lighter so the police wouldn't find it?

911
01:13:39,240 --> 01:13:43,370
He dropped it there, you know, when... Well, that night.

912
01:13:44,040 --> 01:13:46,210
All the police are waiting for...

913
01:13:46,380 --> 01:13:49,630
is one piece of evidence to convict Guy of the murder.

914
01:13:50,710 --> 01:13:52,710
It's had me so worried.

915
01:13:53,220 --> 01:13:55,640
But, of course, I couldn't do it.

916
01:13:55,640 --> 01:13:56,930
It would be too risky.

917
01:13:57,220 --> 01:14:00,430
And besides, it would make me an accessory.

918
01:14:02,560 --> 01:14:06,480
Oh, Miss Morton. I understand how you feel.

919
01:14:07,480 --> 01:14:10,400
Now, if you'll excuse me, I have an urgent appointment.

920
01:14:12,110 --> 01:14:14,030
I really must go now.

921
01:14:46,890 --> 01:14:49,100
He said if the police found your lighter...

922
01:14:49,270 --> 01:14:52,270
that's all they'd need to prove you were at the scene.

923
01:14:52,440 --> 01:14:54,770
That lie about my wanting him to get

924
01:14:54,770 --> 01:14:57,110
it back means he'll put it on that island.

925
01:14:57,280 --> 01:15:00,740
I wanted to help, but I'm afraid all I did is make things worse.

926
01:15:00,950 --> 01:15:03,790
Going there after you did only made him more furious.

927
01:15:04,000 --> 01:15:07,830
No, darling. He said last night he'd think of something.

928
01:15:08,960 --> 01:15:10,250
He certainly has.

929
01:15:10,460 --> 01:15:14,050
Guy, you've got to get to Metcalf before he does.

930
01:15:14,210 --> 01:15:16,760
You haven't time to play. You'd better tell them.

931
01:15:16,970 --> 01:15:18,800
If they announce I won't play,

932
01:15:18,800 --> 01:15:20,640
Hennessy's bound to be suspicious.

933
01:15:20,800 --> 01:15:22,850
He'll keep me from getting near Metcalf.

934
01:15:23,140 --> 01:15:25,140
- Well, then I'll go. - No, Anne.

935
01:15:25,310 --> 01:15:28,520
Stay here and help me give Hennessy the slip after the match.

936
01:15:28,690 --> 01:15:29,980
But that'll be too late.

937
01:15:30,190 --> 01:15:33,900
Didn't Bruno say I wanted him to go there one night after dark?

938
01:15:34,150 --> 01:15:36,190
- Yes. - That's what's in his mind now.

939
01:15:36,490 --> 01:15:38,650
He won't expose himself in broad daylight.

940
01:15:38,910 --> 01:15:41,660
If I can finish in three sets, I'll still...

941
01:15:41,870 --> 01:15:43,830
You're on in a few minutes, Guy.

942
01:15:44,040 --> 01:15:47,000
- Miss Morton. - Okay, Tim. I'll be right there.

943
01:15:48,330 --> 01:15:50,500
This is what I think we'd better do.

944
01:15:50,670 --> 01:15:53,330
If Turley said to, let's pick him up.

945
01:15:53,330 --> 01:15:54,710
Let him have his game first.

946
01:15:54,880 --> 01:15:57,380
First time I ever waited for a murder suspect...

947
01:15:57,550 --> 01:15:59,720
to play tennis before I pulled him in.

948
01:16:00,010 --> 01:16:03,680
The boys at headquarters hear about this, they'll send me...

949
01:16:06,430 --> 01:16:09,100
- Good luck, Guy. - Thanks.

950
01:16:12,860 --> 01:16:14,110
You got it straight.

951
01:16:14,360 --> 01:16:15,900
Make sure Barbara has everything ready

952
01:16:15,900 --> 01:16:18,280
as soon as the third set starts.

953
01:16:41,220 --> 01:16:44,140
Ladies and gentlemen, your attention, please.

954
01:16:44,300 --> 01:16:46,390
This men's singles match will be...

955
01:16:46,600 --> 01:16:50,390
between Mr. Guy Haines and Mr. Fred Reynolds.

956
01:17:01,070 --> 01:17:04,780
On the other hand, Guy Haines is a quiet, methodical player...

957
01:17:04,990 --> 01:17:06,240
almost lackadaisical.

958
01:17:06,490 --> 01:17:10,250
As a rule, he plays slowly between points, well within himself.

959
01:17:10,410 --> 01:17:11,870
Linesmen ready?

960
01:17:12,290 --> 01:17:13,750
Play.

961
01:17:20,510 --> 01:17:22,130
15-love.

962
01:17:28,640 --> 01:17:30,020
30-love.

963
01:17:43,570 --> 01:17:44,820
30-15.

964
01:17:55,790 --> 01:17:57,540
40-15.

965
01:18:21,190 --> 01:18:26,320
Game, Mr. Haines. He leads, one game to love, first set.

966
01:18:37,380 --> 01:18:39,040
Union Station.

967
01:18:45,840 --> 01:18:48,430
Guy Haines is hitting harder, hurrying the play...

968
01:18:48,590 --> 01:18:50,850
taking chances I've never seen him take.

969
01:18:51,010 --> 01:18:54,600
This is a complete reversal of his usual watch-and-wait strategy.

970
01:19:02,980 --> 01:19:05,190
40-30.

971
01:19:50,410 --> 01:19:53,070
Oh, may I have a light, please?

972
01:20:05,630 --> 01:20:07,090
Thanks.

973
01:20:19,640 --> 01:20:23,940
Game, Mr. Haines. He leads, one game to love. Third set.

974
01:20:24,110 --> 01:20:26,610
Guy Haines has caught the first two sets easily.

975
01:20:26,780 --> 01:20:30,150
And if he keeps going, it'll be a straight-set win for him, sure.

976
01:20:31,110 --> 01:20:34,950
Look, if he wins this next set, you'd better have everything ready.

977
01:20:35,160 --> 01:20:37,580
Give the driver this $10.

978
01:20:37,790 --> 01:20:40,080
I wish I understood what this is all about.

979
01:20:40,290 --> 01:20:42,620
I'll tell you that later. Please hurry.

980
01:21:14,320 --> 01:21:15,870
40-love.

981
01:21:30,510 --> 01:21:31,840
Game, Mr. Reynolds.

982
01:21:32,680 --> 01:21:34,550
Advantage, Mr. Reynolds.

983
01:21:35,430 --> 01:21:38,430
Game, Mr. Reynolds. He leads.

984
01:21:38,680 --> 01:21:40,850
I may have spoken too soon when I said...

985
01:21:41,020 --> 01:21:43,020
this match looked to be about over.

986
01:21:43,190 --> 01:21:45,400
Reynolds is up there knocking on the door.

987
01:21:59,700 --> 01:22:01,540
Game, Mr. Reynolds.

988
01:22:01,790 --> 01:22:03,790
Mr. Reynolds wins the third set.

989
01:22:04,210 --> 01:22:06,960
Mr. Haines leading, two sets to one.

990
01:22:41,450 --> 01:22:43,910
The set score is now 2-1. Guy Haines leading...

991
01:22:44,080 --> 01:22:46,080
and we're 10-all in the fourth set.

992
01:22:46,250 --> 01:22:48,460
This match, from a quick victory for Haines,

993
01:22:48,460 --> 01:22:50,210
has turned into a dogfight.

994
01:22:59,930 --> 01:23:01,350
30-all.

995
01:23:03,520 --> 01:23:07,980
Down there. My cigarette case. It's very valuable.

996
01:23:08,270 --> 01:23:11,270
- Down here? - Yes, get that grating up right away.

997
01:23:11,480 --> 01:23:12,730
What's the trouble?

998
01:23:12,900 --> 01:23:15,570
Can't we do something? I dropped my cigarette case.

999
01:23:15,740 --> 01:23:17,470
It mightn't be any good.

1000
01:23:17,470 --> 01:23:18,860
Probably gone down the storm drain.

1001
01:23:19,030 --> 01:23:21,530
- Storm drain? - Maybe it lodged on the edge.

1002
01:23:21,780 --> 01:23:24,950
Don't they have a trap down there, like under a sink?

1003
01:23:25,250 --> 01:23:27,290
Don't just stand here, do something!

1004
01:23:27,540 --> 01:23:29,870
I guess we could phone the city engineer.

1005
01:23:30,040 --> 01:23:32,790
- Worst he could do is tell me to take... - No, look, mister.

1006
01:23:32,960 --> 01:23:36,460
- Relax, mister. - I don't want to relax.

1007
01:23:44,850 --> 01:23:46,520
Game, Mr. Haines.

1008
01:23:46,730 --> 01:23:48,600
Guy Haines is grim and determined.

1009
01:23:48,770 --> 01:23:51,770
He needs but one more game for the entire match.

1010
01:24:24,890 --> 01:24:26,140
40-30.

1011
01:24:26,350 --> 01:24:28,980
Just one point between Haines and the match!

1012
01:24:36,610 --> 01:24:37,860
Deuce.

1013
01:24:52,710 --> 01:24:54,830
Advantage, Mr. Haines.

1014
01:25:03,550 --> 01:25:04,800
Deuce.

1015
01:25:26,580 --> 01:25:28,080
Advantage, Mr. Haines.

1016
01:25:46,220 --> 01:25:49,260
You must think a lot of that, whatever it is.

1017
01:25:53,980 --> 01:25:57,980
Game, set and match, Mr. Haines.

1018
01:26:13,750 --> 01:26:16,120
Taxi's waiting at the entrance.

1019
01:26:24,260 --> 01:26:27,970
Isn't it wonderful? He won. It calls for a celebration.

1020
01:26:28,180 --> 01:26:30,810
- Oh, I'm so sorry. Let me. - It's quite all right.

1021
01:26:30,970 --> 01:26:34,230
No, please. Anne says you must have dinner with us tonight...

1022
01:26:34,390 --> 01:26:36,330
just the family and you and Guy.

1023
01:26:36,330 --> 01:26:37,480
I may be out of town.

1024
01:26:37,690 --> 01:26:39,770
But you'll be with Guy, surely?

1025
01:26:39,940 --> 01:26:41,730
- Hennessy! - I'm sorry. I wish I could.

1026
01:26:41,900 --> 01:26:43,730
Hennessy!

1027
01:26:49,830 --> 01:26:51,160
- You waiting for me? - Yes.

1028
01:26:51,330 --> 01:26:52,660
Penn Station.

1029
01:27:00,840 --> 01:27:02,210
Come on.

1030
01:27:06,840 --> 01:27:08,120
We need your help.

1031
01:27:08,120 --> 01:27:08,840
We're chasing a man.

1032
01:27:09,050 --> 01:27:12,010
Really? How exciting!

1033
01:28:18,710 --> 01:28:20,500
Where'd that man buy a ticket for?

1034
01:28:20,670 --> 01:28:21,960
- Metcalf. - Let's get him.

1035
01:28:22,130 --> 01:28:23,610
That's why we're not.

1036
01:28:23,610 --> 01:28:25,540
Let him go. This may lead to something big.

1037
01:28:25,710 --> 01:28:27,280
We'll phone Metcalf and let

1038
01:28:27,280 --> 01:28:28,550
them take over at that end.

1039
01:28:55,280 --> 01:28:57,580
What time does it get dark around here?

1040
01:29:00,120 --> 01:29:01,960
What's the hurry?

1041
01:29:02,290 --> 01:29:05,460
- What time does it get dark, I said? - Oh, soon enough.

1042
01:29:13,680 --> 01:29:15,800
- Excuse me. - It's quite all right.

1043
01:29:15,970 --> 01:29:19,350
- When do we get into Baltimore? - In about 30 minutes.

1044
01:29:19,640 --> 01:29:21,560
Late again, hmm?

1045
01:29:27,480 --> 01:29:29,820
Nice business he's doing since the murder.

1046
01:29:29,990 --> 01:29:32,070
People wanna see the scene of the crime.

1047
01:29:32,320 --> 01:29:35,280
Come on, come on. Come closer. Here we are.

1048
01:29:35,490 --> 01:29:37,990
It's not a very nice way to make money.

1049
01:29:38,200 --> 01:29:40,950
Well, heck. Boat-keepers gotta eat too.

1050
01:29:41,160 --> 01:29:43,660
Business fell off something terrible for a while.

1051
01:29:43,830 --> 01:29:46,580
The smoochers wouldn't go near the place.

1052
01:29:47,290 --> 01:29:50,130
I'm afraid I don't know what a smoocher is.

1053
01:29:50,630 --> 01:29:53,840
Okay. So I ain't educated.

1054
01:30:30,420 --> 01:30:32,050
Amusement park. Quick.

1055
01:30:40,220 --> 01:30:42,430
He's gone to the amusement park.

1056
01:30:42,890 --> 01:30:46,390
Car 61 F, reporting Guy Haines' arrival at railroad station.

1057
01:30:46,690 --> 01:30:48,850
Suspect bound for amusement park in taxi.

1058
01:32:04,430 --> 01:32:05,850
There he is now.

1059
01:32:06,220 --> 01:32:08,580
Pick up Johnny and follow him.

1060
01:32:08,580 --> 01:32:09,980
We'll go around the other way.

1061
01:32:23,160 --> 01:32:26,120
Keep your eyes open. If you see him, let us know.

1062
01:32:41,800 --> 01:32:43,510
Any more boats?

1063
01:32:51,650 --> 01:32:53,650
It'll be a few minutes' wait.

1064
01:33:24,090 --> 01:33:26,220
Hey, Bruno!

1065
01:33:28,060 --> 01:33:30,850
- Haines, hold it! - Bruno!

1066
01:33:31,060 --> 01:33:32,600
Haines, hold it!

1067
01:33:50,620 --> 01:33:53,080
Bruno! Give me that lighter!

1068
01:33:58,090 --> 01:34:00,750
- Now, stay back, please! - Coming through.

1069
01:34:03,760 --> 01:34:06,430
- He's on there, sir. - He's the one who killed her.

1070
01:34:06,640 --> 01:34:07,930
We know that.

1071
01:34:14,390 --> 01:34:17,440
- Get someone to stop this thing. - I can handle it.

1072
01:34:23,900 --> 01:34:25,200
Hey, be careful! Stop!

1073
01:34:25,410 --> 01:34:28,950
- Well, do you want to do it? - No, I guess he can make it all right.

1074
01:34:50,810 --> 01:34:53,100
My little boy!

1075
01:34:55,310 --> 01:34:57,020
My little boy!

1076
01:37:17,450 --> 01:37:19,750
- Are you all right, Haines? - I think so.

1077
01:37:19,950 --> 01:37:22,960
He says this isn't the man we want. It's the other one.

1078
01:37:23,170 --> 01:37:25,920
- The one he was fighting with. - What do you mean?

1079
01:37:26,090 --> 01:37:27,130
Not Haines?

1080
01:37:27,500 --> 01:37:29,920
- But you pointed him out. - No, I didn't, sir.

1081
01:37:30,090 --> 01:37:33,260
I've never seen this man before. I meant the other one.

1082
01:37:33,470 --> 01:37:36,350
- What's this all about, Haines? - He has my lighter.

1083
01:37:36,640 --> 01:37:40,140
He wanted to plant it on the island to pin the whole thing on me.

1084
01:37:40,310 --> 01:37:43,100
Let me talk to him. Let me show you. Where is he?

1085
01:37:43,310 --> 01:37:45,770
Over here. Take it easy, pal.

1086
01:37:47,650 --> 01:37:50,480
We can't move it. It's too heavy.

1087
01:37:52,780 --> 01:37:55,360
- He's in a bad way. - Can't you get it off him?

1088
01:37:55,660 --> 01:37:58,490
They've done everything they can till the crane comes.

1089
01:37:59,370 --> 01:38:01,370
Hello, Guy.

1090
01:38:03,000 --> 01:38:04,370
Who's this?

1091
01:38:04,620 --> 01:38:07,330
This is Mr. Turley, the chief of police.

1092
01:38:08,920 --> 01:38:10,380
Oh...

1093
01:38:13,090 --> 01:38:16,470
They got you at last, huh, Guy?

1094
01:38:17,680 --> 01:38:21,010
Bruno, can you talk a little?

1095
01:38:22,020 --> 01:38:25,690
Can you tell the chief you have my lighter?

1096
01:38:28,610 --> 01:38:30,320
I haven't got it.

1097
01:38:31,690 --> 01:38:34,030
It's on the island where you left it.

1098
01:38:35,490 --> 01:38:38,490
Bruno, don't keep it up, not at a time like this.

1099
01:38:39,030 --> 01:38:41,200
- You know that... - I'm sorry, Guy.

1100
01:38:41,490 --> 01:38:46,210
I want to help you, but I don't know what I can do.

1101
01:38:47,420 --> 01:38:50,540
Captain Turley, can't I go through his pockets?

1102
01:38:50,750 --> 01:38:53,550
Of course not. Besides, he says he hasn't got it.

1103
01:38:53,760 --> 01:38:55,630
I think he's going.

1104
01:39:00,890 --> 01:39:02,510
He's finished.

1105
01:39:11,940 --> 01:39:13,690
Is this it?

1106
01:39:14,690 --> 01:39:16,240
Well, you were right.

1107
01:39:16,700 --> 01:39:18,700
Better keep this for the time being.

1108
01:39:18,910 --> 01:39:21,120
We'll clear this up in the morning.

1109
01:39:21,120 --> 01:39:23,160
How about staying overnight?

1110
01:39:23,330 --> 01:39:26,910
I imagine there's a lot you want to tell me. Nine o'clock all right?

1111
01:39:27,080 --> 01:39:30,420
Okay, Captain Turley. Thanks.

1112
01:39:33,130 --> 01:39:35,920
- Tell me where there's a telephone. - Up near the entrance.

1113
01:39:36,220 --> 01:39:37,920
Who was he, bud?

1114
01:39:38,640 --> 01:39:40,010
Bruno.

1115
01:39:40,390 --> 01:39:44,180
Bruno Anthony. Very clever fellow.

1116
01:39:50,440 --> 01:39:52,190
Hello?

1117
01:39:52,730 --> 01:39:54,610
Yes, operator, yes.

1118
01:39:55,610 --> 01:39:57,440
Guy?

1119
01:39:59,450 --> 01:40:03,580
Yes, darling, yes. Well, of course I'll be there.

1120
01:40:04,580 --> 01:40:05,870
Goodbye.

1121
01:40:06,870 --> 01:40:10,250
Guy will be back tomorrow. He wants me to bring him some...

1122
01:40:18,130 --> 01:40:21,720
I beg your pardon. But aren't you Guy Haines?

